DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260922T160000
SUMMARY:Postgres as a Coordinator
DESCRIPTION:Toronto Postgres\nTrusting Postgres to store data is one thing
 \; trusting it to coordinate work is another. This talk walks through the 
 design we settled on — commit the durable fact inside the transaction\, 
 fire side effects after commit\, and rebuild the job queue from committed 
 rows so the chain is always the source of truth — and the real failure m
 odes we hit getting there: an advisory-lock self-deadlock that Postgres's 
 own detector couldn't see (half the wait cycle lived in the application)\,
  pg-boss semantics that didn't mean what we assumed\, and MVCC read-then-w
 rite races. For each: the reproduction\, the root cause\, and the structur
 al guard that keeps it from recurring. Because AI agents wrote and tested 
 much of this\, the talk also covers where that helped\, where it quietly d
 idn't\, and the testing discipline we adopted as a result.\nWhat you'll ta
 ke away:\n\n* A durable coordinator pattern: commit-in-transaction\, side-
 effects-after-commit\, queue reconstructable from committed rows\n* Concre
 te concurrency failure modes on Postgres — advisory-lock scope\, pg-boss
  singleton/priority semantics\, MVCC visibility — and how to design them
  out\n* Why the structural guard\, not the one-off fix\, is what makes cor
 rectness stick\n* What building and testing with AI agents got right\, and
  what needed guardrails
